    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mamluk Sultanate was ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a> by slave soldiers, renewed by fresh purchases rather than hereditary succession.</li><li>It halted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mongols">Mongols</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ain-jalut">Ain Jalut</a>, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crusader-states">Crusader states</a> and grew rich on the spice trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>.</li><li>Plague, Portuguese competition at sea and Ottoman guns brought it down, ending with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-conquest-of-egypt">Selim I's conquest of Egypt</a> in 1517.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Though rulers of slave origin, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluk</a> sultans hosted a line of Abbasid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a> from 1261, whose nominal blessing lent them religious legitimacy.</p></aside>
    </section>

